  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM MDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nThe heat continues today, though we will be just a couple of degrees \n\"cooler\" than yesterday. Still, expect to see highs in the mid 90s \nacross much of the area. A RED FLAG WARNING will be in effect for \nthe Central Mountains again today due to critically low relative \nhumidity and breezy winds. We stay just barely below Red Flag \ncriteria throughout the Snake River Plain this afternoon with wind \ngusts around 25 mph to 30 mph. In the same vein as Saturday's \nforecast, there is a very slight chance (again, about 10 percent) \nfor a few virga showers in the South Hills this afternoon. You are \nmore likely to see gusty winds under any radar returns today than \nany rainfall. The ridge of high pressure overhead gets flattened \njust a bit due to troughing developing along the West Coast early in \nthe week ahead, and while this doesn't change the forecast much, it \nwill drop our afternoon highs by just a couple of degrees each \nafternoon through the week ahead. Troughing deepens in the second \nhalf of the week as an area of low pressure moves through \nsouthwestern Canada and that will help drop temperatures into the \nlow 80s by Thursday, Friday, and Saturday afternoons. An increase in \nmoisture looks more likely to hold off until the second half of the \nweek. At this point, we will look forward to any precipitation we \ncan wring out of the atmosphere, but will have to see how models \nchanges over the next several days.",
